{"kind":"request","text":"codex: hugh's second layering finding. Three places encode the same identifier formats independently, so a format change can compile and still break things. He rates it Medium severity, small-to-medium size, low risk. I verified every citation before filing.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY THERE. host/live/live.go:1247 ActorFingerprint is the host's own construction of a full lowercase fingerprint from a public key. gitseq-chess chess.go:555 validActorFingerprint independently re-derives what that means as a validator: hex-decodable, exactly sha256.Size bytes, and equal to its own lowercasing. gitseq-chess cmd/chess/web.go:875 validEventID separately encodes the event-identifier shape, splitting on a hash, requiring two valid object names, and requiring the algorithm segment of each half to match. Nothing connects the three. The host constructs, and two consumers each guess the inverse.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH FIXING AND WHY IT IS NOT URGENT. It is not a defect today; all three agree. It is a defect waiting on a format change, and the failure mode is the bad one: the compiler is silent because nothing is shared, and what breaks is invitation validation and live seat recognition, which are exactly the paths where a wrong answer looks like an ordinary refusal rather than a bug. So it will present as a person unable to join a game, not as a stack trace. That is worth removing, and there is no hurry to remove it.\n\nWHY THIS IS YOURS AND WHY IT IS CORE-ONLY. host owns both formats, so host should own their contract; asking chess to fix its two copies would leave the underlying reason intact and invite a third copy from the next application. But I have deliberately scoped this to core. Chess adopting the exported surface is a separate change to a separate repository under separate review, and bundling them would make one head that neither reviewer can judge alone.\n\nTHE THING I MOST WANT AVOIDED. Do not let consolidation quietly change what validates. These validators sit on untrusted input, and a tightening that rejects a currently-valid identifier breaks live games, while a loosening admits something the fold does not. The conditions therefore ask you to pin current behaviour first and consolidate under it, rather than write the format you would choose today. If you find the current behaviour is itself wrong somewhere, report that rather than fixing it here; it deserves its own decision.\n\nTHE ONE OUTCOME THAT WOULD NOT ANSWER THIS. A shared package that the call sites import while keeping their own encoders looks like consolidation and is not, which is why the conditions ask you to break the shared constructor and show more than one call site fail. My own recent experience is that a shared component can be present and reached and still not be the thing that decides.","body":{"conditions":"Not urgent; sequenced behind the binding-replacement and DNS lanes you already hold, and behind whatever hugh ranks above it. Give host one exported home for actor-fingerprint and event-identifier construction and validation, and make the host-side call sites use it rather than re-deriving the format. Scope is core only: chess adoption is a separate lane in the chess workroom and is not asked for here. The exported surface must be sufficient for an application outside this repository to validate both kinds of identifier without reimplementing the encoding, since that insufficiency is what produced the duplication. Do not change any wire format, any encoding, or any accepted or rejected value: this is consolidation, and a head that alters what validates is a different request. Prove that with a test asserting the exported validators accept and reject exactly what the current implementations do, including the lowercase-hex and length constraints and the event-identifier two-part shape with its matching-object-name rule. Prove the consolidation is real rather than nominal by mutating the shared constructor and confirming failures appear at more than one call site; a change that only renames while leaving the duplicate encoders in place does not answer this. If some duplication turns out to be deliberate because the two formats are genuinely independent and only look alike, say so in the artifact with the reasoning and consolidate only what is actually one format. Exact clean pushed head, per-path artifacts, gofmt, build, vet and go test ./... -count=1 green, independent review. Do not merge.","severity":"normal","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}